{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"TITLE:\"Control of neural circuit formation by leucine-rich repeat proteins.\"","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":50,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"25131359","source":"MED","pmid":"25131359","pmcid":"PMC4189993","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC4189993"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.tins.2014.07.004","title":"Control of neural circuit formation by leucine-rich repeat proteins.","authorString":"de Wit J, Ghosh A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"de Wit J","lastName":"de Wit","firstName":"Joris","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1120-1089"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"VIB Center for the Biology of Disease, 3000 Leuven, Belgium; KU Leuven, Center for Human Genetics, 3000 Leuven, Belgium. Electronic address: CONTACT_EMAIL."}]}},{"fullName":"Ghosh A","lastName":"Ghosh","firstName":"Anirvan","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Neuroscience Discovery, F. Hoffman-La Roche, 4070 Basel, Switzerland."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1120-1089"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"10","volume":"37","journalIssueId":2209365,"dateOfPublication":"2014 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2014,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Trends Neurosci","title":"Trends in neurosciences","essn":"1878-108X","issn":"0166-2236","isoabbreviation":"Trends Neurosci","nlmid":"7808616"},"printPublicationDate":"2014-10-01"},"pubYear":"2014","pageInfo":"539-550","abstractText":"The function of neural circuits depends on the precise connectivity between populations of neurons. Increasing evidence indicates that disruptions in excitatory or inhibitory synapse formation or function lead to excitation/inhibition (E/I) imbalances and contribute to neurodevelopmental and psychiatric disorders. Leucine-rich repeat (LRR)-containing surface proteins have emerged as key organizers of excitatory and inhibitory synapses. Distinct LRR proteins are expressed in different cell types and interact with key pre- and postsynaptic proteins. These protein interaction networks allow LRR proteins to coordinate pre- and postsynaptic elements during synapse formation and differentiation, pathway-specific synapse development, and synaptic plasticity. LRR proteins, therefore, play a critical role in organizing synaptic connections into functional neural circuits, and their dysfunction may contribute to neuropsychiatric disorders.","affiliation":"VIB Center for the Biology of Disease, 3000 Leuven, Belgium; KU Leuven, Center for Human Genetics, 3000 Leuven, Belgium. Electronic address: CONTACT_EMAIL.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Review","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"European Research Council","acronym":"ERC_","grantId":"311083","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NINDS NIH HHS","acronym":"NS","grantId":"R01 NS067216","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NINDS NIH HHS","acronym":"NS","grantId":"R01NS067216","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Neural Pathways","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Synapses","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Synaptic Transmission","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"